      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We can rewrite $235^2-221^2$ as $(235+221)(235-221)=456 \\cdot 14=6384$.\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{235^2-221^2}{14}=\\dfrac{6384}{14}=\\boxed{456}$.\n\nIs that brutal or sum super simple form?\nAlso why is MSG in part D done. Don't they do it right?\n\nProbably 50% of genius comes from curiosity\n\n^what is a genius answer....\n\nMy answer was also just square both sides and cancel the $14$.\n\nAnd it was a lot easier when you have $235$ and $221$\n\nAfter all, $235-221=14$, so $\\frac{\\boxed{6384}}{\\boxed{14}=5\\boxed{26}}$\n\nError\n$235^2-221^2=(235+221)(235-221)=\\boxed{456\\cdot 14=6384}. $ \n\n(You don't have to square both terms to get this out)\n\n$x=235^2-221^2$\n$y=14$\n$x-456y=0$\n$y=0$ or $x=0$\n\nI thought that's much simpler\n\nWe have $235^2-221^2=(235+221)(235-221)=456\\cdot 14$. Easily, we can get that $456\\cdot 14=6384$.\n\nCan we use difference of squares to find the work or may I write an excel and find out the result?\n\nsubtract $456*14$ from them\n\nThe idea is:\n\n$$\\frac{235^2-221^2}{14} = \\frac{(x-y)(x+y)}{x-y}$$\n\nSince $x = 235$ and $y = 221$, that's $235$ and that should be obvious. But you can see how this works when $y$ is multiplied by, $\\frac{x+y}{x-y}$, since it just gets canceled out\nNote how with radicals you can write: $$\\frac {a^2 - b^2}{c} = \\frac {a}{c} * (a-b)\\cdot (a+b) \\cdot \\frac {b^2}{b^2} = \\frac {(a+b)(a-b)(a^2) + (b+a)(b^2) - b^2}{c}$$ Verify it as true.\nThis could help in more difficult problems or simpler ones as well.\n\nyou guys are so smart!\nIt's easy when you see it. So just subtract it out!\n\nSAT Math  Questions: 51\nhttp://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&cid=47&year=2017\nKey Board Math forum",
